      "text": "First, we need to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of the numbers 18 and 42. The GCD of two numbers is the largest number that divides both of them without leaving a remainder. One way to find the GCD is to use the Euclidean algorithm, which involves repeated division.\n\n1. Divide 42 by 18:\n\\[\n42 = 18 \\cdot 2 + 6\n\\]\nThis can be written as:\n\\[\n42 - 18 \\cdot 2 = 6\n\\]\nSo, the remainder is 6.\n\n2. Now, we divide 18 by 6:\n\\[\n18 = 6 \\cdot 3 + 0\n\\]\nThe remainder is 0, which means that 6 is the GCD of 18 and 42.\n\nNext, we find the least common multiple (LCM) of 18 and 42. The LCM of two numbers is the smallest positive number that is a multiple of both of them. The relationship between the GCD and the LCM of two numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(a, b) = \\frac{a \\cdot b}{\\text{GCD}(a, b)}\n\\]\nUsing the numbers 18 and 42:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(18, 42) = \\frac{18 \\cdot 42}{\\text{GCD}(18, 42)}\n\\]\nSince we have already found that \\(\\text{GCD}(18, 42) = 6\\):\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(18, 42) = \\frac{18 \\cdot 42}{6} = \\frac{756}{6} = 126\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the product of the GCD and the LCM:\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(18, 42) \\cdot \\text{LCM}(18, 42) = 6 \\cdot 126 = 756\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of 18 and 42 is \\(\\boxed{756}\\).",
