      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the product of the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of 18 and 42, we can use the relationship between the GCD and LCM of two numbers. The relationship is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(a, b) \\times \\text{LCM}(a, b) = a \\times b\n\\]\n\nIn this problem, \\(a = 18\\) and \\(b = 42\\). First, we need to find the GCD of 18 and 42. We can do this using the Euclidean algorithm.\n\n1. **Euclidean Algorithm to Find GCD:**\n\n   - Divide 42 by 18 and find the remainder:\n     \\[\n     42 \\div 18 = 2 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 6\n     \\]\n     So, \\(42 = 18 \\times 2 + 6\\).\n\n   - Next, divide 18 by the remainder 6:\n     \\[\n     18 \\div 6 = 3 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 0\n     \\]\n     So, \\(18 = 6 \\times 3 + 0\\).\n\n   Since the remainder is now 0, the GCD is the last non-zero remainder, which is 6. Therefore, \\(\\text{GCD}(18, 42) = 6\\).\n\n2. **Calculate the LCM Using the Relationship:**\n\n   Using the relationship between GCD and LCM:\n   \\[\n   \\text{LCM}(18, 42) = \\frac{a \\times b}{\\text{GCD}(a, b)}\n   \\]\n   Substituting the values we have:\n   \\[\n   \\text{LCM}(18, 42) = \\frac{18 \\times 42}{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Calculate the product of 18 and 42:\n   \\[\n   18 \\times 42 = 756\n   \\]\n\n   Now, divide by the GCD:\n   \\[\n   \\text{LCM}(18, 42) = \\frac{756}{6} = 126\n   \\]\n\n3. **Find the Product of the GCD and the LCM:**\n\n   Finally, we need to find the product of the GCD and the LCM:\n   \\[\n   \\text{GCD}(18, 42) \\times \\text{LCM}(18, 42) = 6 \\times 126\n   \\]\n\n   Calculate the product:\n   \\[\n   6 \\times 126 = 756\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the product of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of 18 and 42 is \\(\\boxed{756}\\).",
